# PuddleDuck Python runtime

The runtime wheel contains the Python implementation of the PuddleDuck
protobuf API, robot-side services, policy support, recording tools, and the
`broadcast_state` operator web interface.

The complete Python command reference is generated in the Doxygen manual:

- [Python protobuf API](https://openbdx.github.io/docs/PuddleDuck/python_protobuf_api.html)
- [Build and deploy](https://openbdx.github.io/docs/PuddleDuck/firmware_deployment.html)

## Build wheels

From the repository root:

```bash
make runtime-cpu
make runtime-gpu
# or both
make runtime
```

The wheels are written to `runtime/dist/`. During a wheel build,
`grpcio-tools` compiles the canonical schemas in `../protos` into Python
`*_pb2.py` modules inside the wheel. Do not hand-edit generated protobuf Python
modules.

A normal robot upload installs a freshly built CPU runtime together with the
matching firmware:

```bash
BDX_UPLOAD_HOST=<duck>.local make puddleduck-v4 VERBOSE=1 UPLOAD=1
```

Deploy the current `bdx-overlay` first when Linux helpers, service units, web
permissions, or updater API expectations have changed.

## Device endpoints

The runtime normally communicates through:

- `/dev/puddleduck_ctrl` — framed protobuf command/response channel;
- `/dev/puddleduck_ops` — operational state and diagnostics reports;
- `/dev/puddleduck` — firmware console and log stream.

Only one process should own the control channel at a time. Stop a running policy
or service before opening `/dev/puddleduck_ctrl` directly from an interactive
Python script.

## Web administration

`broadcast_state` serves the body operator UI on port 8080. Caddy exposes the
normal HTTPS endpoint through the duck identity and the head access point.
Settings and runtime features use persistent operator files under the `duck`
home directory, including:

- `~/duck_actions.yaml` — controller-triggered process actions;
- `~/duck_triggers.yaml` — lightweight sound and QuackHead triggers;
- `~/duck_control.yaml` — DuckDrive and DuckSense configuration;
- `~/controllers/*.yaml` — controller definitions and user overrides;
- `~/emotes/*.json` — operator-owned emotes and embedded Duckmation projects;
- `~/.config/puddleduck/viewer.json` — selected Duck model;
- `~/sounds/` — body sound library, synchronized to the head on demand.

On a BDX body, the `duck` home resolves to persistent `/data/home/duck`, so
these settings survive A/B rootfs updates.

The Log page streams `/dev/puddleduck`, accepts one-line firmware console
commands, and exposes runtime verbose categories through the protobuf control
channel. Firmware and runtime must be rebuilt from the same protobuf sources
after changing that API.

Operational details for authentication, HTTPS, recordings, audio, controllers,
emotes, assets, diagnostics, and firmware updates are indexed in the
[operations manual](https://openbdx.github.io/docs/PuddleDuck/operations.html).
